	affiliations = {Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering, University of Chicago, Chicago, 60637, IL, United States; Department of Physics, University of Massachusetts-Amherst, Amherst, 01003, MA, United States},
	abstract = {One-way nonreciprocal interactions between two quantum systems are typically described by a cascaded quantum master equation, and rely on an effective breaking of time-reversal symmetry (TRS) as well as the balancing of coherent and dissipative interactions. Here, we present a new approach for obtaining nonreciprocal quantum interactions that is completely distinct from cascaded quantum systems, and that does not in general require broken TRS. Our method relies on a local gauge symmetry present in any Markovian Lindblad master equation. This new kind of quantum nonreciprocity has many implications, including a new mechanism for performing dissipative steady-state unitary gate operations on a target quantum system. We also introduce a new, extremely general quantum-information-based metric for quantifying quantum nonreciprocity. © 2023 authors. Published by the American Physical Society.},
