	affiliations = {Huygens Laboratorium, Rijksuniversiteit Leiden, 2300 RA Leiden, Postbus 9504, Netherlands},
	abstract = {It is known that a quantum master equation that describes dissipative relaxation (such as spontaneous decay) can be separated in a part that describes spontaneous jumps and a jump-free part. The jump-free part of the evolution is determined by a non-Hermitian Hamiltonian H, which acts on state vectors, rather than on density operators. We identify a class of systems for which the complete evolution is entirely determined by the jump-free evolution, which is governed by H. Although this class is restricted, it contains a sizable number of systems of physical significance. Among these are two-, three-, and four-state atoms. The method is used to find explicit expressions of time-dependent expectation values in terms of the complex eigenvalues of H. As a first application we evaluate exactly the photon statistics in the driven system. As a second example of the method, we treat the configuration of a V system driven by two standing waves. This system constitutes an effective beam splitter. © 1995 The American Physical Society.},
